            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Sargon</strong><br>\nSargon of Akkad, also known as Sargon the Great, was born an outsider to the royal lineage. His origins are shrouded in mystery and legend says he was the son of a gardener. The story of his birth is interesting and full of intrigue, as it is said that he was placed in a basket on the river by his mother and later discovered by a royal gardener who raised him.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nSargon's rise to power was marked by his service to Ur-Zababa, the king of Kish. However, circumstances led to Sargon becoming a king himself. His reign began around 2334 BC after a series of events that historians believe included a palace coup against Ur-Zababa. Sargon's ascent marked the start of the Akkadian Empire, which he would expand greatly.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Conquests</strong><br>\nSargon was a formidable military leader who created one of the first known empires by conquering the Sumerian city-states. He led his army across the Fertile Crescent and beyond, extending Akkadian rule to distant lands. His conquests included large territories in Mesopotamia, parts of the Levant, and Anatolia, forging a wide-reaching empire.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Governance and Administration</strong><br>\nTo govern his empire, Sargon established a new administrative system that replaced local rulers with his own loyal officials. This ensured that all the regions of his empire were controlled directly from the capital city, Akkad. His bureaucratic system was advanced for its time and set a precedent for future empires.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Akkad, the Capital City</strong><br>\nSargon built the city of Akkad as the seat of his empire, though its precise location remains unknown to archaeologists. The city became a bustling hub of commerce, culture, and governance. It was from Akkad that Sargon ruled and oversaw his vast empire, and the city's name became synonymous with his dynasty.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Sargon's Army</strong><br>\nSargon's army was powerful and innovative. It is credited with being one of the first to use composite bows and siege warfare techniques. Sargon often boasted of his military prowess, claiming that no one could stand against his army. His military campaigns were vital in expanding and maintaining his empire.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
